module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.QuasiFinite
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Spaces.GenericPointsFiniteComponents

/-!
# Finitely many irreducible components, from the target of a quasi-finite morphism to its source

Finiteness of the set of irreducible components does **not** transport from the target of a
finite surjective morphism of finite presentation to its source. What does transport it is the
density, in the source, of the inverse image of the generic points of the target: that inverse
image is then a finite dense subset, and a space with a finite dense subset is the union of the
finitely many closures of its points.

## Main results

* `Set.Finite.irreducibleComponents_of_dense`: a topological space with a finite dense subset has
  finitely many irreducible components.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.finite_irreducibleComponents_of_dense_preimage_genericPoints`: the source of
  a quasi-compact morphism, locally quasi-finite over a target with finitely many irreducible
  components, has finitely many irreducible components as soon as the inverse image of the
  generic points of the target is dense. A finite morphism supplies both typeclasses.

## The hypothesis is needed: finiteness and surjectivity do not suffice

Let `A` be a commutative ring with finitely many minimal primes and `I ⊆ A` an ideal, and put
`B = A × A ⧸ I`. Then `B` is generated by two elements as an `A`-module, so `Spec B ⟶ Spec A` is
finite; it is surjective because `A` is a direct factor of `B`; and `Spec B` is the disjoint union
of `Spec A` and `Spec (A ⧸ I)`, so its irreducible components are those of `Spec A` together with
those of `Spec (A ⧸ I)`, i.e. the minimal primes over `I`. So the question is whether a closed
subscheme of a scheme with finitely many irreducible components can have infinitely many, and
over a non-Noetherian ring it can:

* `A = k[x₀, x₁, x₂, …]` a polynomial ring in countably many variables and
  `I = (xᵢxⱼ : i ≠ j)`. `A` is a domain, so `Spec A` is irreducible. Each `pᵢ = (xⱼ : j ≠ i)` is
  prime with `I ⊆ pᵢ`, and is minimal over `I`: a prime `q` with `I ⊆ q ⊆ pᵢ` omitting some `xⱼ`
  contains every `xₖ`, `k ≠ j`, hence contains `pⱼ ⊄ pᵢ`. So `I` has infinitely many minimal
  primes.
* With finite presentation as well: `A` the ring of all algebraic integers and `I = (2)`. Then
  `B = A[T] ⧸ (T² - T, 2T)` is a finitely presented `A`-algebra and a finite `A`-module, `A` is a
  domain, and the primes of `A` over `2` are infinitely many and pairwise incomparable, hence are
  infinitely many minimal primes over `I`.

In both cases the inverse image of the generic point of `Spec A` misses the second sheet
entirely, which is exactly the failure of the hypothesis below. Neither counterexample is
formalized here.

So the argument "every minimal prime of `B` lies over a minimal prime of `A`, and the fibre over
each is finite" is not available: its first half is false for a finite surjective ring map, as
`A = k[x]`, `B = k[x] × k` already shows — the minimal prime `0 × k` lies over `(x)`.

## What transports component finiteness to the source

The hypothesis used here is implied by flatness, which makes the generic points of the source lie
over those of the target, and that recovers the ascent half of
`AlgebraicGeometry.finiteIrreducibleComponents_isLocal_fppf` (Stacks 0BAL). Density of a
*quasi-compact open* with finitely many irreducible components is the other sufficient condition,
and that one is already available as
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.finite_irreducibleComponents_of_denseRange`, applied to the inclusion of
the open; the lemma below is that statement for a finite dense subset instead.

The opposite direction — from the source to the target — is
`ncard_irreducibleComponents_le_of_surjective` (Stacks 0GM2) for a surjection and
`AlgebraicGeometry.Space.finite_irreducibleComponents_of_denseRange` for a map of dense range.
-/

@[expose] public section

open TopologicalSpace

universe u

/-- A topological space with a finite dense subset has finitely many irreducible components: the
images of the irreducible components of the subset are finitely many irreducible sets with dense
union. -/
theorem Set.Finite.irreducibleComponents_of_dense {X : Type*} [TopologicalSpace X] {s : Set X}
    (hs : s.Finite) (hd : Dense s) : (irreducibleComponents X).Finite :=
  have := hs.to_subtype
  AlgebraicGeometry.Space.finite_irreducibleComponents_of_denseRange
    (f := (Subtype.val : s → X)) continuous_subtype_val
    (by simpa only [DenseRange, Subtype.range_coe_subtype, Set.ofPred_mem_eq] using hd)
    (Set.toFinite _)

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- **Finiteness of the set of irreducible components passes from the target of a quasi-finite
morphism to its source when the inverse image of the generic points is dense.** Let `f : Y ⟶ X`
be a quasi-compact and locally quasi-finite morphism of schemes — a finite morphism, for
instance — and assume `X` has finitely many irreducible components. If the inverse image of the
set of generic points of `X` is dense in `Y`, then `Y` has finitely many irreducible components.

The generic points of `X` are finitely many, their inverse image is therefore finite, and a space
with a finite dense subset has finitely many irreducible components.

Density is not automatic for a finite surjective morphism of finite presentation, and without it
the conclusion fails; see the module documentation. -/
theorem finite_irreducibleComponents_of_dense_preimage_genericPoints {X Y : Scheme.{u}}
    (f : Y ⟶ X) [LocallyQuasiFinite f] [QuasiCompact f]
    (hX : (irreducibleComponents X).Finite) (hdense : Dense (f ⁻¹' genericPoints X)) :
    (irreducibleComponents Y).Finite :=
  (f.finite_preimage (genericPoints.finite hX)).irreducibleComponents_of_dense hdense

end AlgebraicGeometry

end
